1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,562
At the Corner Cafe, Asha adds tax to every
price.

2
00:00:03,582 --> 00:00:08,730
She needs three lines: an empty list, a
loop, and append with the new price.

3
00:00:08,767 --> 00:00:10,351
Then names in capitals.

4
00:00:10,371 --> 00:00:12,164
Then only the vegan items.

5
00:00:12,184 --> 00:00:16,487
Asha thinks: another empty list, another
loop, another append.

6
00:00:16,507 --> 00:00:18,440
There must be a shorter way!

7
00:00:19,053 --> 00:00:22,561
This is Code Quest, Chapter 14:
comprehensions.

8
00:00:22,581 --> 00:00:24,963
One line that builds a new list.

9
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:30,184
And an AI assistant will rewrite Asha's
loop in one line, and promise the same

10
00:00:30,204 --> 00:00:30,651
output.

11
00:00:30,671 --> 00:00:32,319
Will it keep its promise?

12
00:00:32,339 --> 00:00:33,320
You will check.

13
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:38,265
So here is today's mission: the menu board
for the Corner Cafe.

14
00:00:38,285 --> 00:00:42,700
Lines 2 to 5 hold each item, its price,
and whether it is vegan.

15
00:00:42,733 --> 00:00:45,858
Lines 6, 7 and 8 are three comprehensions.

16
00:00:45,878 --> 00:00:49,003
Each one builds something new in one line.

17
00:00:49,033 --> 00:00:51,617
And the printing stays in a normal loop.

18
00:00:51,637 --> 00:00:52,723
You will see why.

19
00:00:52,743 --> 00:00:53,699
Let's build it.

20
00:00:54,283 --> 00:00:59,326
Write list comprehensions, filter them,
build dictionaries the same way, and know

21
00:00:59,346 --> 00:01:00,763
when a loop is clearer.

22
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:06,035
And ask an AI to rewrite a loop, then
prove that the output stayed the same.

23
00:01:06,617 --> 00:01:11,502
A comprehension builds a new list or
dictionary from another collection, in one

24
00:01:11,522 --> 00:01:11,812
line.

25
00:01:11,832 --> 00:01:14,607
It says what you want, not how to collect
it.

26
00:01:14,633 --> 00:01:18,447
Data tools and reports use comprehensions
all the time.

27
00:01:18,467 --> 00:01:20,886
But they do not replace every loop.

28
00:01:20,906 --> 00:01:23,883
Part of this chapter is learning to
choose.

29
00:01:24,483 --> 00:01:28,143
A list comprehension has three parts
inside square brackets.

30
00:01:28,163 --> 00:01:29,983
Start in the middle: the loop.

31
00:01:30,003 --> 00:01:32,743
For price in prices, exactly like a for
loop.

32
00:01:32,767 --> 00:01:36,182
On the left is the new value, made for
each item.

33
00:01:36,202 --> 00:01:39,547
Here, the price with tax, rounded to two
places.

34
00:01:39,567 --> 00:01:42,161
And on the right, an optional filter.

35
00:01:42,181 --> 00:01:45,271
Keep the item only if the condition is
True.

36
00:01:45,291 --> 00:01:46,260
No empty list.

37
00:01:46,280 --> 00:01:46,967
No append.

38
00:01:47,587 --> 00:01:51,203
Think of a comprehension as a recipe card
for a new list.

39
00:01:51,223 --> 00:01:55,287
For each price, if it is under 4, write
down the price with tax.

40
00:01:55,333 --> 00:01:59,880
Unlike a real recipe card, it cannot do
other jobs on the way.

41
00:01:59,900 --> 00:02:02,384
No printing, and no running total.

42
00:02:02,404 --> 00:02:04,446
It only builds the new list.

43
00:02:05,053 --> 00:02:05,830
Quick check.

44
00:02:05,850 --> 00:02:09,214
In this comprehension, which part is the
new value?

45
00:02:20,580 --> 00:02:20,703
C.

46
00:02:20,723 --> 00:02:21,775
Name dot upper.

47
00:02:21,795 --> 00:02:26,704
The new value comes first, on the left,
and it is made for each item.

48
00:02:27,320 --> 00:02:28,437
Take a breather.

49
00:02:28,457 --> 00:02:30,498
Here is what you know so far.

50
00:02:30,518 --> 00:02:33,697
Next, Asha rewrites her tax loop in one
line.

51
00:02:38,450 --> 00:02:43,520
Step 1 is the loop you saw at the start:
an empty list, a loop, and append, with

52
00:02:43,540 --> 00:02:45,810
round to two places, from Chapter 2.

53
00:02:45,833 --> 00:02:46,356
Step 2.

54
00:02:46,376 --> 00:02:49,457
Line 3 builds the same list in one line.

55
00:02:49,477 --> 00:02:53,023
The new value first, then for price in
prices.

56
00:02:53,067 --> 00:02:54,828
Line 4 prints the new list.

57
00:02:54,848 --> 00:02:59,577
Line 5 prints the length of the new list,
and the length of the old one.

58
00:03:10,947 --> 00:03:17,847
Line 4 prints exactly the same list as the
loop: 3.85, 4.4, 4.68, and 3.03.

59
00:03:17,867 --> 00:03:20,510
And line 5 prints 4 and 4.

60
00:03:20,530 --> 00:03:26,863
Without a filter, the new list has one
item for each old item.

61
00:03:27,450 --> 00:03:27,941
Step 3.

62
00:03:27,961 --> 00:03:30,569
The new value can be any expression.

63
00:03:30,589 --> 00:03:34,220
Line 2 holds the menu names, all in small
letters.

64
00:03:34,267 --> 00:03:38,647
Line 3 uses title, the string method from
Chapter 3.

65
00:03:38,667 --> 00:03:42,540
Line 4 uses len, so each new item is a
number.

66
00:03:53,913 --> 00:03:59,783
Line 5 prints the names with capital
letters: Tea, Coffee, Carrot Cake, Scone.

67
00:03:59,833 --> 00:04:03,566
Line 6 prints the lengths: 3, 6, 11 and 5.

68
00:04:03,586 --> 00:04:06,783
The space in carrot cake counts too.

69
00:04:06,833 --> 00:04:09,774
And line 7 shows the old list, unchanged.

70
00:04:09,794 --> 00:04:12,662
A comprehension always makes a new list.

71
00:04:13,250 --> 00:04:13,762
Step 4.

72
00:04:13,782 --> 00:04:18,622
Each tuple holds a name, a price, and
whether the item is vegan.

73
00:04:18,642 --> 00:04:22,420
The loop part unpacks all three, as in
Chapter 13.

74
00:04:22,467 --> 00:04:24,465
An if at the end is a filter.

75
00:04:24,485 --> 00:04:28,849
An item goes into the new list only when
the condition is True.

76
00:04:28,869 --> 00:04:30,867
Line 6 keeps the vegan names.

77
00:04:30,900 --> 00:04:34,966
Line 7 keeps the names of the items that
cost less than 4.

78
00:04:46,347 --> 00:04:47,547
Tea and Scone.

79
00:04:47,567 --> 00:04:51,817
Coffee costs exactly 4, and 4 is not less
than 4.

80
00:04:51,867 --> 00:04:55,202
And line 8 prints the vegan names: Tea and
Coffee.

81
00:04:55,222 --> 00:04:58,088
With a filter, the new list can be
shorter.

82
00:04:58,683 --> 00:04:59,242
Step 5.

83
00:04:59,262 --> 00:05:02,797
Line 3 has an if at the end, so it
filters.

84
00:05:02,817 --> 00:05:04,863
Only prices under 4 stay.

85
00:05:04,900 --> 00:05:06,091
Line 4 is different.

86
00:05:06,111 --> 00:05:08,694
The if and else come first, before the
for.

87
00:05:08,714 --> 00:05:12,810
That chooses a value for every item: snack
under 4, otherwise treat.

88
00:05:12,833 --> 00:05:17,990
This form must have an else, because every
item needs a value.

89
00:05:29,347 --> 00:05:29,715
Four.

90
00:05:29,735 --> 00:05:33,827
One label for each price: snack, treat,
treat, snack.

91
00:05:33,867 --> 00:05:36,355
But line 5 prints only two prices.

92
00:05:36,375 --> 00:05:38,863
The filter made that list shorter.

93
00:05:39,453 --> 00:05:40,255
Quick check.

94
00:05:40,275 --> 00:05:43,678
Which comprehension keeps only the prices
under 4?

95
00:05:55,047 --> 00:05:55,176
B.

96
00:05:55,196 --> 00:05:57,185
The filter goes at the end.

97
00:05:57,205 --> 00:06:01,726
Option A keeps every item, and puts 0 in
place of the others.

98
00:06:02,320 --> 00:06:02,930
Breather.

99
00:06:02,950 --> 00:06:04,262
Relax for a moment.

100
00:06:04,282 --> 00:06:07,484
Next, the same idea builds a whole
dictionary.

101
00:06:12,217 --> 00:06:12,788
Step 6.

102
00:06:12,808 --> 00:06:16,677
Line 2 is the menu dictionary from Chapter
12.

103
00:06:16,700 --> 00:06:21,134
Line 3 uses curly brackets with a key, a
colon, and a value.

104
00:06:21,154 --> 00:06:23,955
That makes a dictionary comprehension.

105
00:06:23,975 --> 00:06:26,330
Items gives each name and price.

106
00:06:26,367 --> 00:06:29,398
Line 5 reads cake from the new dictionary.

107
00:06:29,418 --> 00:06:32,014
Line 6 reads cake from the old menu.

108
00:06:43,380 --> 00:06:46,893
4.68, the cake with tax, and then 4.25.

109
00:06:46,913 --> 00:06:49,430
The old menu did not change.

110
00:06:49,467 --> 00:06:55,433
Line 4 shows the whole new dictionary: the
same keys, with new values.

111
00:06:56,017 --> 00:06:56,450
Step 7.

112
00:06:56,470 --> 00:07:00,527
Today, items of 4 or more cost 0.50 less,
and they get a label.

113
00:07:00,547 --> 00:07:01,887
Lines 5 to 7 do that.

114
00:07:01,933 --> 00:07:05,959
Line 8 prints each item, and line 9 adds
it to a total.

115
00:07:05,979 --> 00:07:08,313
That is four jobs for each item.

116
00:07:08,333 --> 00:07:12,327
A comprehension builds one new list and
nothing else.

117
00:07:12,347 --> 00:07:15,583
Here, a normal loop is much easier to
read.

118
00:07:15,633 --> 00:07:17,114
So the rule is simple.

119
00:07:17,134 --> 00:07:20,387
One new list or dictionary: use a
comprehension.

120
00:07:20,407 --> 00:07:24,138
Several steps, a print, or a running
total: use a loop.

121
00:07:24,717 --> 00:07:26,029
Back to the mission.

122
00:07:26,049 --> 00:07:30,227
Line 6 builds a dictionary: each name,
with its price plus tax.

123
00:07:30,267 --> 00:07:32,619
Line 7 filters the vegan names.

124
00:07:32,639 --> 00:07:37,287
Line 8 filters the board, so it compares
the prices with tax.

125
00:07:37,333 --> 00:07:42,066
Lines 9 and 10 print the board with a
normal loop, because printing is not

126
00:07:42,086 --> 00:07:43,093
building a list.

127
00:07:43,133 --> 00:07:47,227
Line 12 prints the names whose price with
tax is under 4.

128
00:07:58,580 --> 00:07:59,771
Tea and Scone.

129
00:07:59,791 --> 00:08:03,230
With tax, Tea is 3.85 and Scone is 3.03.

130
00:08:03,250 --> 00:08:06,170
Coffee becomes 4.40, so it is out.

131
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:09,777
Line 11 prints the vegan items, Tea and
Coffee.

132
00:08:09,797 --> 00:08:12,149
Three comprehensions, one loop.

133
00:08:12,169 --> 00:08:13,450
Mission complete!

134
00:08:13,500 --> 00:08:17,572
Next, Asha wants a loop of her own in one
line.

135
00:08:17,592 --> 00:08:21,576
She asks an AI assistant, one stage at a
time.

136
00:08:22,150 --> 00:08:25,117
This chapter's AI skill is safe
refactoring.

137
00:08:25,137 --> 00:08:30,480
To refactor means to change how code is
written, without changing what it does.

138
00:08:30,500 --> 00:08:36,216
And you prove it: run both versions on the
same data, and compare every line.

139
00:08:36,820 --> 00:08:37,837
Stage 1: plan.

140
00:08:37,857 --> 00:08:41,170
Asha's loop adds tax to the vegan items
only.

141
00:08:41,190 --> 00:08:47,170
Before any rewrite, she asks the AI what
the new line must keep exactly the same.

142
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:52,840
The AI gives a checklist: only vegan
items, the same order, the same rounding,

143
00:08:52,860 --> 00:08:53,638
and a list.

144
00:08:53,658 --> 00:08:55,525
Asha keeps this checklist.

145
00:08:56,120 --> 00:08:57,332
Stage 2: write.

146
00:08:57,352 --> 00:08:59,632
Asha wrote the loop herself.

147
00:08:59,652 --> 00:09:04,890
She asks the AI to predict its exact
output, without running it.

148
00:09:04,933 --> 00:09:08,862
The AI predicts 3.85 and 4.4, only Tea and
Coffee.

149
00:09:08,882 --> 00:09:12,336
Asha predicted the same, and the run
agrees.

150
00:09:12,953 --> 00:09:14,269
Stage 3: generate.

151
00:09:14,289 --> 00:09:20,281
Asha asks the AI to rewrite her loop as a
list comprehension, keep the output the

152
00:09:20,301 --> 00:09:22,433
same, and say what it prints.

153
00:09:22,467 --> 00:09:29,117
The AI gives one line, and promises 3.85
and 4.4, the same as the loop.

154
00:09:29,720 --> 00:09:32,317
Here is the AI's line, on line 6.

155
00:09:32,337 --> 00:09:35,727
Compare it with the checklist from stage
1.

156
00:09:47,080 --> 00:09:47,268
No.

157
00:09:47,288 --> 00:09:50,043
Point 1 on the checklist was the filter.

158
00:09:50,063 --> 00:09:53,650
Line 6 has no if at the end, so it keeps
every item.

159
00:09:53,700 --> 00:09:56,669
Asha runs it: four prices instead of two.

160
00:09:56,689 --> 00:09:59,220
The cake and the scone got tax too.

161
00:09:59,240 --> 00:10:03,083
The AI sounded sure, and it dropped part
of her loop.

162
00:10:03,687 --> 00:10:04,829
Stage 4: test.

163
00:10:04,849 --> 00:10:10,307
Asha asks for three test cases, with the
expected result for each.

164
00:10:10,333 --> 00:10:16,366
A normal menu, a menu with no vegan items,
and two vegan items with the same price.

165
00:10:16,987 --> 00:10:22,431
Lines 2 to 6 keep the three cases in a
dictionary: a name, and a small menu for

166
00:10:22,451 --> 00:10:22,777
each.

167
00:10:22,800 --> 00:10:26,620
For each case, lines 8 to 11 run Asha's
loop.

168
00:10:26,667 --> 00:10:28,792
Line 12 runs the AI's line.

169
00:10:28,812 --> 00:10:35,229
Line 13 uses the same if and else from
step 5: it prints pass when both lists are

170
00:10:35,249 --> 00:10:37,216
the same, otherwise FAIL.

171
00:10:48,580 --> 00:10:51,937
The normal menu and the menu with no vegan
items.

172
00:10:51,957 --> 00:10:54,970
Both get a scone price they should not
have.

173
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:59,158
The same-price case passes, because both
items are vegan.

174
00:10:59,178 --> 00:11:01,504
One passing case proves nothing.

175
00:11:01,524 --> 00:11:02,457
Run them all.

176
00:11:03,053 --> 00:11:04,125
Stage 5: debug.

177
00:11:04,145 --> 00:11:09,146
Asha tries to add the filter herself, and
puts the if before the for.

178
00:11:09,166 --> 00:11:11,547
Python stops with a syntax error.

179
00:11:11,567 --> 00:11:14,894
She pastes the line and the full error
report.

180
00:11:15,487 --> 00:11:21,004
The AI explains: an if before the for
chooses a value for every item, so it

181
00:11:21,024 --> 00:11:22,038
needs an else.

182
00:11:22,058 --> 00:11:23,220
Remember step 5.

183
00:11:23,240 --> 00:11:25,878
To filter, move if vegan to the end.

184
00:11:26,487 --> 00:11:29,323
Asha moves the if to the end, on line 12.

185
00:11:29,343 --> 00:11:35,037
Then she runs every case again, because a
fix can break a case that worked before.

186
00:11:35,067 --> 00:11:36,646
Normal: 3.85, pass.

187
00:11:36,666 --> 00:11:39,171
No vegan: an empty list, pass.

188
00:11:39,191 --> 00:11:40,602
Same price: pass.

189
00:11:40,622 --> 00:11:42,370
All three cases pass.

190
00:11:42,987 --> 00:11:44,227
Stage 6: improve.

191
00:11:44,247 --> 00:11:48,082
Asha asks for one change that makes her
line better.

192
00:11:48,102 --> 00:11:51,937
And she adds: the output must stay exactly
the same.

193
00:11:51,967 --> 00:11:56,803
The AI suggests curly brackets, because a
set is faster to search.

194
00:11:56,823 --> 00:11:58,863
It promises the same output.

195
00:11:59,453 --> 00:12:01,544
Asha does not trust it yet.

196
00:12:01,564 --> 00:12:05,140
Line 12 now has curly brackets, with no
colon.

197
00:12:05,160 --> 00:12:06,703
She runs every case.

198
00:12:18,080 --> 00:12:18,263
No.

199
00:12:18,283 --> 00:12:19,415
Every case fails.

200
00:12:19,435 --> 00:12:23,617
Curly brackets made a set, and a set is
never equal to a list.

201
00:12:23,637 --> 00:12:26,870
Set, with empty round brackets, is an
empty set.

202
00:12:26,900 --> 00:12:31,587
And look at the same-price case: the two
equal prices merged into one.

203
00:12:31,607 --> 00:12:33,470
A set keeps each value once.

204
00:12:33,500 --> 00:12:37,560
The AI broke her rule: the output did not
stay the same.

205
00:12:37,580 --> 00:12:39,819
Asha keeps her square brackets.

206
00:12:40,420 --> 00:12:41,210
Quick check.

207
00:12:41,230 --> 00:12:43,639
An AI rewrote your loop as one line.

208
00:12:43,659 --> 00:12:46,608
How do you prove the output stayed the
same?

209
00:12:57,980 --> 00:12:58,108
A.

210
00:12:58,128 --> 00:13:02,914
Run both versions on the same test cases,
and compare every line.

211
00:13:02,934 --> 00:13:04,614
A promise is not proof.

212
00:13:05,220 --> 00:13:05,830
Breather.

213
00:13:05,850 --> 00:13:11,217
Asha kept a checklist, ran three cases,
and caught two AI mistakes: a missing

214
00:13:11,237 --> 00:13:13,106
filter, and curly brackets.

215
00:13:13,126 --> 00:13:16,464
Next, the comprehension mistakes beginners
make.

216
00:13:21,217 --> 00:13:23,574
Mistake 1 is the AI's improvement.

217
00:13:23,594 --> 00:13:27,349
Curly brackets with no colon make a set
comprehension.

218
00:13:27,369 --> 00:13:29,237
Line 4 shows the type: set.

219
00:13:29,267 --> 00:13:33,307
A set has no fixed order, so line 5 prints
it sorted.

220
00:13:33,327 --> 00:13:34,685
There is no error.

221
00:13:34,705 --> 00:13:40,967
But the list had 3.5 twice, and the set
kept 3.85 only once: 3 items instead of 4.

222
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,508
The fix: square brackets for a list.

223
00:13:43,528 --> 00:13:47,371
Curly brackets with key, colon, value for
a dictionary.

224
00:13:47,987 --> 00:13:48,927
Now you try.

225
00:13:48,947 --> 00:13:52,447
Look closely at line 3, and remember step
5.

226
00:14:03,813 --> 00:14:06,714
A filter at the end cannot have an else.

227
00:14:06,734 --> 00:14:09,853
Python stops with a syntax error on line
3.

228
00:14:09,900 --> 00:14:15,249
The fix: to choose a value for every item,
move the if and else to the start.

229
00:14:15,269 --> 00:14:17,132
To filter, remove the else.

230
00:14:17,753 --> 00:14:18,440
Mistake 3.

231
00:14:18,460 --> 00:14:22,541
Print shows text on the screen, but it
gives back nothing.

232
00:14:22,561 --> 00:14:24,733
Python calls that nothing None.

233
00:14:24,767 --> 00:14:28,213
Line 3 builds a list from three calls to
print.

234
00:14:28,233 --> 00:14:29,983
Line 4 prints that list.

235
00:14:41,347 --> 00:14:42,470
None, None, None.

236
00:14:42,490 --> 00:14:45,697
The names were printed, but the list is
useless.

237
00:14:45,733 --> 00:14:49,556
The fix: when the job is printing, use a
normal for loop.

238
00:14:50,153 --> 00:14:50,956
Mistake 4.

239
00:14:50,976 --> 00:14:53,424
Line 3 adds tax with no round.

240
00:14:53,444 --> 00:14:58,443
Remember Chapter 2: some floats are stored
with a tiny error.

241
00:14:58,467 --> 00:15:02,268
The fix: round inside the comprehension,
as line 5 does.

242
00:15:02,288 --> 00:15:06,500
Or format the price with two decimal
places when you print it.

243
00:15:07,120 --> 00:15:07,971
Last breather.

244
00:15:07,991 --> 00:15:12,391
Now you know the four comprehension
mistakes that catch most beginners.

245
00:15:17,117 --> 00:15:17,491
First.

246
00:15:17,511 --> 00:15:18,871
Asha has five orders.

247
00:15:18,891 --> 00:15:23,078
Write a comprehension that keeps only the
teas, then count them.

248
00:15:23,098 --> 00:15:26,168
Could you count them without making a new
list?

249
00:15:37,547 --> 00:15:39,947
Write it after the video, and run it.

250
00:15:40,553 --> 00:15:41,013
Second.

251
00:15:41,033 --> 00:15:46,088
A comprehension can have two for parts,
such as every size of every drink.

252
00:15:46,108 --> 00:15:49,105
Predict the order of the items, then run
it.

253
00:16:00,480 --> 00:16:03,319
Compare your prediction with the real
output.

254
00:16:03,917 --> 00:16:04,674
Let's recap.

255
00:16:04,694 --> 00:16:09,017
A list comprehension: the new value, then
for, then an optional if.

256
00:16:09,067 --> 00:16:10,675
An if at the end filters.

257
00:16:10,695 --> 00:16:14,517
An if and else at the start chooses a
value for every item.

258
00:16:14,567 --> 00:16:17,518
Curly brackets with a colon build a
dictionary.

259
00:16:17,538 --> 00:16:19,857
And the old collection never changes.

260
00:16:19,900 --> 00:16:25,612
Use a loop for several steps, and with AI,
run both versions on the same cases.

261
00:16:26,220 --> 00:16:27,248
One last check.

262
00:16:27,268 --> 00:16:29,622
What does this comprehension give?

263
00:16:40,980 --> 00:16:41,114
D.

264
00:16:41,134 --> 00:16:41,729
4 and 6.

265
00:16:41,749 --> 00:16:46,421
The filter keeps 2 and 3, and the new
value doubles each one.

266
00:16:47,020 --> 00:16:48,335
Mission complete!

267
00:16:48,355 --> 00:16:51,789
You earned 100 XP and the Transformer
badge.

268
00:16:54,017 --> 00:16:57,545
Now it is your turn: eight tasks, worth
170 XP.

269
00:16:57,565 --> 00:17:03,284
Three warm-up tasks at 10 XP, and three
apply tasks at 20, including a stock

270
00:17:03,304 --> 00:17:03,737
check.

271
00:17:03,767 --> 00:17:10,517
And two stretch tasks, worth 40 each: a
vegan menu board, and an AI's refactor to

272
00:17:10,537 --> 00:17:11,019
check.

273
00:17:11,039 --> 00:17:14,530
Try each one before you open the solution.

274
00:17:15,117 --> 00:17:19,722
Asha now knows lists, dictionaries,
tuples, sets and comprehensions.

275
00:17:19,742 --> 00:17:25,028
But every day, she still adds up the
Corner Cafe's costs on paper: milk, cups,

276
00:17:25,048 --> 00:17:25,777
tea leaves.

277
00:17:25,800 --> 00:17:30,977
In Chapter 15, Boss Level 3, you will
build an expense tracker that does it for

278
00:17:30,997 --> 00:17:31,240
her.

279
00:17:31,267 --> 00:17:32,945
Do the warm-up tasks now.

280
00:17:32,965 --> 00:17:34,847
They take about ten minutes.

281
00:17:34,867 --> 00:17:36,675
See you in Chapter 15.
